  "verbalization": "Insulin Degludec, a long-acting insulin analog, is linked to Diabetes Mellitus (disease) through a series of indirect pathways involving Diabetic Ketoacidosis. The evidence suggests that Insulin Degludec is used to treat Diabetic Ketoacidosis, which in turn is indicated for Insulin Glulisine and Insulin Glargine. These insulins are known to be used in the management of diabetes, though their direct indication for diabetes is less clear. The integration of these pathways implies that Insulin Degludec may exert a role in the pathophysiology of diabetes by influencing metabolic processes associated with diabetic ketoacidosis. The recurring mechanism involves the use of insulin analogs to manage hyperglycemia, suggesting a potential role in diabetes management through metabolic regulation. The evidence supports a relationship between Insulin Degludec and diabetes, though the exact mechanism remains partially inferred, requiring further study to clarify the direct causal link."
